1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,560
But if in fact there are deadweight costs or are an issue,

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:06,440
they were that the winner of the conflict might really be nobody.

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,280
It might be that there's just a giant bonfire of wealth,

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:13,240
and there's great misery that is caused by the conflicts.

5
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:17,340
Once you have something like a 30 years war and Europe is laid waste,

6
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:19,760
then finally non-economists may start saying,

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:21,720
you know there really was no winner here.

8
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:25,120
But, although even there not technically true,

9
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:29,240
what about the most fanatical people who thought it was better,

10
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:32,720
that it's better to murder people than let their souls be destroyed?

11
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:36,160
Or said, look if we by killing the people of the wrong religion,

12
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,640
we prevented them from spreading the poison to others and destroying their souls.

13
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:44,560
So, but still, the idea that a lot of resources get burned up and are

14
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:48,800
captured by nobody in a very serious war people eventually get the idea.

15
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:52,240
But when it's a less destructive war then who won?

16
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,680
Who came out of this looking great?

17
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:57,880
And again the answer according to the concept of deadweight costs,

18
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:01,000
maybe actually there's just a great deal of destruction.

19
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,120
So, in other words if you have a fixed pie of resources all conflict benefits somebody.

20
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:08,600
And if there is a variable pie of resources,

21
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:11,960
conflict might actually just be generally disruptive.

22
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:15,120
All right. Now, again as a matter of just definition,

23
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:18,440
reducing deadweight costs is automatically a Calrix improvements.

24
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:20,520
And then if a situation is Calrix efficient,

25
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,240
then deadweight costs are zero automatically.

26
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:30,960
All right. So, questions about Calrix versus Pareto efficiency?

27
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:34,800
Any questions about either of these concepts?

28
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,080
Right. And again, so, and remind me,

29
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:41,080
which one is tends to appear on homework problems and journal articles again?

30
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:42,200
Pareto.

31
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:45,440
Which one is used to analyze actual policies in the real world?

32
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:46,160
Calrix.

33
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:51,880
Calrix. Again, normally under the name cost-benefit analysis.

34
00:01:51,880 --> 00:02:01,880
Okay. So, moving on.

35
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:05,240
So, Calrix efficiency is based on dollar valuations.

36
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:08,600
It's not based on utility in any sense,

37
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:10,640
and it's not based upon happiness in

38
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,200
the rich psychological sense that you probably know about.

39
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:16,880
Right. So, if you ever heard someone who's happy,

40
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,360
and if they appear to be,

41
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:20,240
if they have a very miserable face on,

42
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:22,640
then you generally say you don't look happy.

43
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:25,080
Right. Your action is generally not,

44
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:26,800
well, you did what you want most wanted to do,

45
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:28,040
so you must be happy.

46
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:30,560
Or at least, you must be happier than you would have

47
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,400
been given any other possible choice that was available to you.

48
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,480
Right. If you were an economics for a while,

49
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:36,880
you're very tempted to say that.

50
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:39,560
And yet, if there's a person who was happy before they made the choice,

51
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:40,720
with a smile on their face,

52
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,480
and afterwards they're crying,

53
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:45,800
you say, maybe they're not happy after doing this.

54
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,400
Maybe they didn't do the thing that actually made them happiest.

55
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:52,080
Right. And how could that possibly be?

56
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:57,440
All right. Well, there is a difference between willingness to pay and human happiness.

57
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:00,440
Hopefully, you have noticed this at some point in your life.

58
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:02,720
There's many different counter examples,

59
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:05,080
but one thing, so you can know that I'm willing to pay

60
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:09,120
$100 for something without knowing anything at all about how happy it makes me.

61
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:12,680
So, here my favorite example is buying kitchen granite.

62
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:14,720
Kidding, I've seen granite kitchens,

63
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:16,880
they have granite. That granite costs a lot,

64
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:18,680
so $10,000 for a kitchen.

65
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:21,240
Now, I know a lot of people have granite,

66
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:24,440
and I've never seen anyone ever smile at the granite.

67
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:26,200
I've never seen anyone go,

68
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,480
this is my granite, I love my granite so much.

69
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:32,920
My beautiful, beautiful, smooth granite,

70
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:36,200
and it has this special cut on the side.

71
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,720
It's so cute, I just love you.

72
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:43,760
Right. So, I've never heard anyone express these emotions or,

73
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:47,600
I've seen movies in other languages where people have granite,

74
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,680
and in no language has anyone ever talked to their granite with this kind of emotion.

75
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:57,880
Whereas, I've seen people get an ice cream cone that costs way less than the granite,

76
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:01,360
and they're super happy, ice cream, and they're really happy.

77
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:06,800
So, there must be some disconnect between willingness to pay and happiness.

78
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:08,480
Seems pretty obvious,

79
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:10,680
unless you think the ice cream people are pretending,

80
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:14,000
well, hi, this ice cream is not nearly as happiness-degrading as granite,

81
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,440
but I'm going to pretend I like it a lot more than granite.

82
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:21,120
So, and people look at the granite,

83
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:23,960
my God, granite completes me as a human being,

84
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:25,760
but it would be weird if people knew that.

85
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:30,560
So, I pretend to be really cool about my granite.

86
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,920
Seems that there are actually large disparities between

87
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:38,040
willingness to pay and this emotion of happiness

88
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:41,760
that human beings are very well able to recognize in other people.

89
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:47,040
It's very cross-culturally easy to recognize how happy somebody is.

90
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:49,000
Again, you may say, well,

91
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
there are some cultures that frown upon the expression of happiness, that may be so.

92
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,760
Although, don't you just think they're really unhappy?

93
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:58,680
You see cultures where people just never smile.

94
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:01,800
Aren't you only seeing, maybe the culture says don't smile,

95
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,400
but maybe they're actually just miserable here.

96
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,840
Is that possible too? Seems possible. Yes.

97
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,200
On the granite versus ice cream question,

98
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:12,720
can we analyze that in terms of marginal utility?

99
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:16,360
So, the ice cream has a much higher marginal utility versus the granite?

100
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:20,040
So, think about a person who eats ice cream every day and they own the granite every day.

101
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,400
Yes. I mean, like the ice cream,

102
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:24,920
like every ice cream they seem to be enjoying it more than every day of granite.

103
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:26,960
I mean, I have never,

104
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:29,920
ever seen a person smile at granite in the kitchen.

105
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:32,000
A person ever eat ice cream every day?

106
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:32,560
Yes.

107
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:33,400
Have that reaction?

108
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:36,440
Yes. Well, I mean, that actually is so, let's see.

109
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:37,120
Yes.

110
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:38,480
People eat ice cream every day.

111
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:40,440
I know a number of people eat ice cream,

112
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:43,160
any chance they get each time, ice cream.

113
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:48,480
Yes. The fact that they smile at the ice cream ever and never smile at the granite,

114
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:50,200
which costs so much more.

115
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:55,760
That's a lifetime supply of ice cream you have just put into a surface.

116
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:59,600
So, where did the cost of ice cream come from?

117
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:02,440
All right. Right. And then, in addition,

118
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:03,520
just think about, say,

119
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:07,400
you have high happiness and low happiness friends that you have.

120
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:11,320
It's possible that the friends you have who seem to smile a lot are really not all that happy,

121
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:15,480
and the friends you have who seem to be kind of miserable all the time are super happy inside.

122
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:17,720
But it's really hard to believe that.

123
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:22,000
So, yeah, maybe they're just really private people and they smile a lot in private,

124
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,680
or maybe this is like this,

125
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:27,120
is them feeling jubilation.

126
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:32,280
It's possible, but it seems kind of hard to believe, at least to me.

127
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:34,480
So, you know something that makes me very happy,

128
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,640
you do have low willingness to pay for it.

129
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:41,200
In fact, there are a number of things that most people will say make them very happy,

